---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import ccmReports from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/doctor-ccm-reports.png';
import patientDashboard from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/patient-dashboard.png';
import patientProfile from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/doctor-patient-profile.png';
import healthPlan from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/doctor-health-plan.png';
import messages from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/doctor-messages.png';
import encounterNotes from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/vcc-encounter-notes.png';
import billingCodes from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/vcc-billing-codes.png';
import mobileHome from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/mobile-home.png';
import mobileCarePlan from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/mobile-care-plan.png';
import mobileMedications from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/mobile-medications.png';
import mobileGoals from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/mobile-goals.png';
import mobileMetrics from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/mobile-health-metrics.png';
import mobileGlucose from '../../assets/projects/clearpath/mobile-glucose-trend.png';

const desktopSizes = '(min-width: 1000px) 960px, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const pairSizes = '(min-width: 900px) 580px, calc(100vw - 32px)';
const phoneSizes = '(min-width: 760px) 230px, (min-width: 600px) 30vw, 64vw';
const widths = [800, 1200, 1600, 2200];

const appDayScreens = [
  { src: mobileHome, alt: 'Patient app home screen greeting the patient, with their care plan, tonight’s appointment with their care coordinator and a Join Call button, health metrics, and goal counts' },
  { src: mobileCarePlan, alt: 'My Care Plan screen opening with emergency guidance, then what changed in the latest review, when to contact the care team, and who is on it' },
  { src: mobileMedications, alt: 'My Medications screen for today, grouped into morning, evening, and bedtime, with each dose checked off as taken except the bedtime one' },
];

const appProgressScreens = [
  { src: mobileGoals, alt: 'My Doctor’s Health Plan screen with overall progress and each goal labeled In Progress, Completed, or Not Satisfied beside its colored dot' },
  { src: mobileMetrics, alt: 'Health Metrics screen with a three-month range selected and category filters, showing BMI, weight, body fat, and blood glucose cards with trend lines' },
  { src: mobileGlucose, alt: 'Blood Glucose Trend screen with latest, average, and range values, a 7.5 percent improvement over three months, a line chart, and the list of readings' },
];

const appRules = [
  'Health metrics went from 5 to 15, grouped into the web’s four categories. Each one declares which direction is better, so a change is only called an improvement when it is one. Sleep has a healthy range rather than a maximum, so it is never labeled better or worse.',
  'Color is never the only cue: every change also carries an arrow and a label, and units come straight from the API, never converted on the device.',
  'The doctor’s health plan and the patient’s personal goals stay separate features on separate endpoints, and each loads and fails on its own, so one outage can’t blank the other.',
  'The care plan comes from a patient-safe projection the server builds. Its contents never reach a log, console, or crash report, and a malformed plan still renders what it can instead of an error where the emergency guidance belongs.',
];

const portals = [
  { name: 'Virtual Care Coordinator (VCC)', text: 'The primary operational portal: encounter documentation, monthly CCM reporting, quality and population-health tracking, scheduling, patient assignments, and consent workflows.' },
  { name: 'Doctor', text: 'Oversight and sign-off: reviewing CCM summaries and care-plan updates from coordinators, managing prescriptions, and handling escalations.' },
  { name: 'Patient', text: 'A view-only portal where patients follow their own care plan, prescriptions, health metrics, and personal goals between visits.' },
  { name: 'Admin', text: 'Internal staff tooling on its own API: account management, analytics, cross-portal CCM oversight, and audit logs.' },
];

const ccmMonth = [
  { name: 'Consent', text: 'Patients enroll in CCM by consenting in the app, or through a secure link sent by email.' },
  { name: 'Document encounters', text: 'Coordinators log each patient contact through the month. The note adapts to how the contact went: a full clinical note, an onboarding intake, or a short record of an outreach attempt.' },
  { name: 'Submit for review', text: 'At month’s end the coordinator submits a summary, which moves from In Progress to Pending Review.' },
  { name: 'Physician sign-off', text: 'The doctor approves the report or returns it as Needs Revision. Approved reports and encounters export to PDF.' },
];

const safeguards = [
  'PHI never appears in URLs, query strings, or client-side logs. Every log goes through a logger that redacts it.',
  'Anything sensitive held in browser storage is purged on logout and on session timeout.',
  'Multi-factor authentication is mandatory for clinicians, and every portal warns before timing out an idle session.',
  'An expired token is refreshed and the request retried once; if that fails, the user is signed out cleanly.',
  'Role checks in the interface exist for usability only. The API is the security boundary, and the frontend is built on that assumption.',
  'Care-team chat is text-only between coordinators and doctors, and is designed so no PHI travels over the websocket.',
  'Content Security Policy headers, encryption in transit and at rest, and audit logging for sensitive actions.',
  'An AWS audit trail across every region, with tamper-evident log digests and seven-year retention: a year past HIPAA’s six-year documentation requirement. Every read of PHI storage is logged, while the public website bucket logs only writes, which keeps the cost negligible without losing the record that matters.',
];
---

<CaseStudy
  name="ClearPath Virtual Health"
  description="Case study: how Sām Kazemi architected ClearPath Virtual Health, a production HIPAA-compliant CCM/RPM platform with four role-based portals."
  summary="A production HIPAA-compliant chronic care management (CCM) and remote patient monitoring (RPM) platform that helps patients stay on track and helps providers deliver better care between visits."
  role="Lead Software Engineer: architecture, frontend, cloud, team lead"
  org="MedManage Solutions"
  period="Sep 2025 – Present"
  status="Live in production"
  live="https://clearpathvirtualhealth.com/"
  liveLabel="clearpathvirtualhealth.com"
  stack={[
    'React',
    'JavaScript',
    'React Router',
    'Tailwind CSS',
    'Material UI',
    'Lucide React',
    'Flutter',
    'Dart',
    'REST APIs (bearer tokens)',
    'Laravel Echo + Pusher',
    'RBAC',
    'MFA',
    'jsPDF',
    'Jest',
    'React Testing Library',
    'ESLint',
    'GitHub Actions',
    'AWS S3',
    'AWS CloudFront',
    'AWS ECS Fargate',
    'AWS CloudTrail',
    'HIPAA',
    'CPT / ICD-10',
  ]}
>
  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p>
    <h2>Chronic care happens between visits</h2>
    <p>
      Patients living with chronic conditions need support every month, not only when they are in
      the exam room. Medicare’s Chronic Care Management program pays for that ongoing coordination,
      but only when the work is documented precisely, time is tracked accurately, and a physician
      signs off.
    </p>
    <p>
      The platform had to serve four very different users with strict boundaries between what each
      of them can see and do, hold up to HIPAA, and produce billing records that stand up to
      Medicare, all without adding work to clinicians’ days.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">His role</p>
    <h2>Architected from the ground up</h2>
    <p>
      Sām architected and built ClearPath from the ground up and leads its ongoing development: designing
      scalable features, improving clinical workflows with the care team, and managing a distributed
      engineering team. He owns the React frontend across all four portals and its integration with the
      platform’s Laravel API, and works across the AWS infrastructure and delivery pipeline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">What he built</p>
    <h2>Four portals, one platform</h2>
    <p>
      ClearPath spans 30+ components across four role-based portals, each scoped to exactly what that
      role needs. Each role signs in with its own token and downloads only its own portal.
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-flow">
      {
        portals.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <strong>{p.name}</strong>
              <span>{p.text}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={ccmReports}
        alt="Doctor portal CCM Reports page for September 2026: counts of total, ready-to-sign, signed, and needs-revision reports, with each patient's report ready to sign or send back"
        widths={widths}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The doctor portal’s monthly review: every coordinator-submitted CCM report, signed one at a
        time or all at once. Every name and reading in these screenshots is synthetic sample data.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={patientDashboard}
        alt="Patient portal dashboard greeting the patient, with their care team, today's appointment with their care coordinator, and their health metrics"
        widths={widths}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The patient portal: a read-only view of the patient’s care team, appointments, health
        metrics, goals, and medications.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="cs-features">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="lock" /></span>
        <h3>Security and compliance</h3>
        <p>
          HIPAA-compliant session management, multi-factor authentication, and audit logging, with
          token-authenticated REST APIs and role-based access control at every boundary.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="file-text" /></span>
        <h3>Monthly CCM billing reports</h3>
        <p>
          Encounter documentation, CPT and ICD-10 code management, physician sign-off workflows, and
          PDF export, directly supporting Medicare reimbursement compliance.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="users" /></span>
        <h3>Care coordination in real time</h3>
        <p>
          Patient records, care plans, and medication lists stay in sync across portals as they
          change, with full medication history kept for compliance.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="message-square" /></span>
        <h3>Care-team messaging</h3>
        <p>
          Live chat between coordinators and doctors, with @-mentions that link a conversation to
          the patient it is about.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="heart-pulse" /></span>
        <h3>Shared health plans and metrics</h3>
        <p>
          A goal checklist doctors and coordinators build together, with red, yellow, and green status
          that never relies on color alone. Patient goals can track a health metric from baseline to
          target.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="search" /></span>
        <h3>Fast, keyboard-first interface</h3>
        <p>
          A Ctrl/Cmd + K search across patients, notes, and appointments, accessible notifications
          and dialogs, and route-level code splitting to keep each portal light.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="cs-shot cs-shot--wide">
      <div class="cs-pair">
        <Screenshot
          src={patientProfile}
          alt="Doctor's view of a patient profile with health metric cards for BMI, weight, body fat, blood glucose, and blood pressure, each with a trend chart, above the weekly encounter details"
          widths={[600, 900, 1200, 1600]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
        <Screenshot
          src={healthPlan}
          alt="Doctor's Health Plan checklist with goals marked done, in progress, or not done, above the patient's personal goals with progress toward a tracked metric"
          widths={[600, 900, 1200, 1600]}
          sizes={pairSizes}
          class="cs-screen"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        One shared chart component shows a patient’s metrics in every portal. The Doctor’s Health
        Plan pairs every red, yellow, or green status with a text label, and personal goals track a
        metric from baseline to target.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={messages}
        alt="Messages page with a conversation list and a thread between the doctor and a care coordinator about a patient, whose name is an @-mention link"
        widths={widths}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Care-team messaging between a doctor and a coordinator. The @-mention links the conversation
        to the patient’s record.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The mobile app</p>
    <h2>The patient portal, on iOS and Android</h2>
    <p>
      Patients can also follow their care from a Flutter app for iOS and Android. Sām brought the
      team’s app to parity with the web patient portal, taking every field, value set, and endpoint
      from the web implementation rather than inventing any, so the two can’t drift apart. He also
      added the patient’s care plan.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <div class="cs-phones">
        {appDayScreens.map((s) => <Screenshot src={s.src} alt={s.alt} widths={[300, 500, 780]} sizes={phoneSizes} class="cs-screen cs-phone" />)}
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The same patient as the web screenshots, on the evening of their monthly check-in: the home
        screen with the call’s join link, the care plan with emergency guidance first, and today’s
        medications, checked off as they’re taken.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ul class="check-list">
      {appRules.map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <div class="cs-phones">
        {appProgressScreens.map((s) => <Screenshot src={s.src} alt={s.alt} widths={[300, 500, 780]} sizes={phoneSizes} class="cs-screen cs-phone" />)}
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The doctor’s health plan with a text label beside every colored status, health metrics
        grouped by category, and three months of blood glucose readings.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The core workflow</p>
    <h2>A month of chronic care, start to finish</h2>
    <p>
      CCM is billed monthly, and the whole month has to hold up on paper. ClearPath models it as a
      clear lifecycle that coordinators and doctors move through together.
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-flow">
      {
        ccmMonth.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <strong>{s.name}</strong>
              <span>{s.text}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={encounterNotes}
        alt="Coordinator's encounter note for a CCM call: contact outcome set to CCM Encounter, call start and end times totaling 34 minutes, consent confirmed, and vitals with trend sparklines"
        widths={widths}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        A coordinator documenting a CCM call. The contact outcome decides which form appears, and
        every vital shows its trend beside the field.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The rules are enforced, not suggested. Complex CCM can’t be approved without the documentation
      it requires, including medical decision-making (MDM), and that gate is never relaxed. Billing
      codes mirror the server’s calculation and can be overridden by hand, but the interface never
      invents or silently corrects a code. Health-metric units come straight from the API and are
      never converted in the browser.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-shot">
      <Screenshot
        src={billingCodes}
        alt="Billing Codes (CPT) card with 99490 checked automatically for a 34-minute standard CCM call, and the remaining codes available as manual overrides"
        widths={widths}
        sizes={desktopSizes}
        class="cs-screen"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Billing codes follow the documented time: 34 minutes of standard CCM checks 99490 only,
        because 99439 needs 40. Any code can still be toggled by hand.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Security</p>
    <h2>Designed around protected health information</h2>
    <p>
      ClearPath handles PHI, so the frontend treats every piece of it as something to minimize. A few
      of the rules the codebase is built on:
    </p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      {safeguards.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Delivery</p>
    <h2>Releases that don’t break anyone</h2>
    <p>
      The React frontend is served from S3 and CloudFront, in front of a Laravel API on ECS Fargate
      and PostgreSQL on RDS. Every push runs a dependency audit, a zero-warning lint, and the test
      suite, and nothing is released until that passes. The release process encodes a few hard-won
      lessons.
    </p>
    <div class="cs-features">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="cloud" /></span>
        <h3>Caching that matches the files</h3>
        <p>
          Content-hashed bundles are cached for a year, files with stable names stay revalidatable,
          and the HTML is never cached, so a new favicon or sitemap reaches returning visitors instead
          of hiding in their browser cache.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="rocket" /></span>
        <h3>No broken open tabs</h3>
        <p>
          Old bundles survive a release, because a tab that is already open still asks for them. A
          pruning script removes an asset only once it is both gone from the live build and more than
          30 days old, dry-runs by default, and refuses to run against a build that isn’t live.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="lock" /></span>
        <h3>No source maps, ever</h3>
        <p>
          On a public origin, a source map would publish the complete client source of a PHI
          platform. The build inspects its own output and fails if one survives, rather than trusting
          a config flag.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="check-circle" /></span>
        <h3>Verified, not assumed</h3>
        <p>
          After every release, the bundle served from production is checked against the build that
          was just uploaded, so a stale cache or a bad upload can’t pass silently.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Impact</p>
    <h2>Measurable results for the business and for patients</h2>
    <ul class="cs-metrics">
      <li><strong>200%+</strong><span>increase in recurring revenue</span></li>
      <li><strong>15%</strong><span>fewer missed appointments</span></li>
      <li><strong>40%</strong><span>less administrative overhead</span></li>
      <li><strong>35%</strong><span>faster deployments</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">What he learned</p>
    <h2>In healthcare, correctness is the feature</h2>
    <p>
      A billing code, a timestamp, or a permission check that is slightly wrong is not a cosmetic
      bug. It affects patient care and Medicare compliance. Building ClearPath taught him to design for
      that: strict role boundaries, defensive handling of every piece of data, and a delivery process
      where nothing ships without passing its checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also taught him where to spend testing effort. ClearPath’s tests concentrate where a
      regression costs the most: authentication and routing, session timeout, the API and security
      layer, CCM and billing logic, and PHI handling.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
